### HTTP listen address

The chart configures the HTTP listen address via `.Values.http` list.
Each entry in the list configures a separate HTTP listener.
The primary listener (marked with `primary: true`) is used for Pod health checks and Service port generation.

To enable TLS on the HTTP listener:

```yaml
http:
  - name: https
    primary: true
    value: :8427
    tls: true
    tlsCertFile: /path/to/tls.crt
    tlsKeyFile: /path/to/tls.key
```

To expose an additional listener on a different port:

```yaml
http:
  - name: http
    primary: true
    value: :8427
  - name: https
    value: :8428
    tls: true
    tlsCertFile: /path/to/tls.crt
    tlsKeyFile: /path/to/tls.key
```

## Resource Naming

By default, this chart uses release-name based resource naming for historical reasons and
backwards compatibility with existing deployments.

> [!WARNING]
> For new deployments, it is recommended to set `useLegacyNaming: false` to use operator-style
> names (e.g. `vmauth-<release>`). This aligns resource names with the
> [VictoriaMetrics operator](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/) convention and
> simplifies future migration to operator-managed resources.

### VictoriaMetrics operator

With `operator.enabled: true` the chart renders a [VMAuth](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/resources/vmauth/) resource
instead of its own Deployment, Service, ServiceAccount and PodDisruptionBudget,
and the [VictoriaMetrics operator](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/) manages the workload.
The operator must already be installed in the cluster. Operator mode always uses operator-style names, regardless of `useLegacyNaming`.

The VMAuth is named after the release (or `fullnameOverride`), so the operator creates `vmauth-<release>` objects.
`ingressInternal` is still rendered by the chart and targets it. Instead of rendering its own scrape object, the chart lets the operator create the VMServiceScrape when `serviceMonitor.enabled` is enabled, and disables it otherwise.

The spec is built from chart values: image, replicas, resources, scheduling, env, extra args, volumes, containers,
pod metadata, security context, HTTP listen port, Service settings, service account and license. In addition:

- `networkPolicy` ingress and egress rules become the resource `networkPolicy`, and the operator creates the NetworkPolicy. Its labels and annotations can't be set;
- `ingress` becomes the VMAuth `ingress` (first host and TLS entry as `host`/`tlsSecretName`, other hosts as `extraRules`) and `route` becomes `httpRoute`; the operator creates both;
- the chart keeps rendering `config` (or `configRaw`) into a Secret named `vmauth-<release>-config`, and the VMAuth uses it as `externalConfig`, so vmauth runs with the same config. `secretName` is referenced the same way;
- `podDisruptionBudget` becomes a VMAuth field, and the operator creates the PodDisruptionBudget;
- only `mode: deployment` is supported by the VMAuth.

Fields that have no chart value, or need a different value, go to `operator.spec`, which is deep-merged over the generated spec:

```yaml
operator:
  enabled: true
  spec:
    useProxyProtocol: true
```

Rendering fails for settings that have no operator equivalent, with a message pointing to `operator.spec`.
Probes keep the operator defaults. Switching a release to `operator.enabled: true` recreates its pods.

